WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.193
One photograph of a court hall.

00:00:02.193 --> 00:00:04.808
Over half a million Gaussians, made in one pass.

00:00:05.188 --> 00:00:08.218
Swing a little to the side, and the scene holds.

00:00:08.218 --> 00:00:10.643
Swing further, and black opens behind every pillar.

00:00:13.915 --> 00:00:14.744
Hi, I'm Bokeh.

00:00:14.744 --> 00:00:17.231
A photo tells a network how big things look.

00:00:17.231 --> 00:00:19.719
How far away they are comes from somewhere else.

00:00:21.742 --> 00:00:25.513
Apple's Depth Pro 2 predicts metric depth for photos and video, sharp

00:00:25.513 --> 00:00:28.341
at the edges and steady from frame to frame.

00:00:28.721 --> 00:00:33.914
It comes in four sizes, from twenty-four million to eight hundred seventy-two million parameters.

00:00:35.158 --> 00:00:39.130
One transformer runs over every frame at the input's own resolution.

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:41.658
No tiles, no squashing into a square.

00:00:42.038 --> 00:00:45.787
After the first third of its blocks, frames start attending to each other.

00:00:45.787 --> 00:00:47.517
That is what keeps video steady.

00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:51.348
Single-frame methods are sharp and jittery.

00:00:51.348 --> 00:00:53.329
Video methods are steady and blurry.

00:00:53.329 --> 00:00:56.300
Depth Pro 2 sits alone at the top right.

00:00:58.325 --> 00:01:01.477
The network outputs inverse depth divided by the focal length.

00:01:01.477 --> 00:01:05.260
That is how big things look, which is all one photo holds.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.218
Multiply by a focal length to get metres.

00:01:08.218 --> 00:01:12.025
Thirty percent too long, and the room comes out thirty percent deeper.

00:01:12.445 --> 00:01:14.731
Too short, and it squashes toward the camera.

00:01:17.045 --> 00:01:18.282
The width never moves.

00:01:18.282 --> 00:01:21.375
Sideways position doesn't depend on the focal length at all.

00:01:21.375 --> 00:01:24.159
A wrong one stretches the scene along the view.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:28.275
Here it is on Depth Pro 2's real depth for the

00:01:28.275 --> 00:01:29.212
hall, seen from above.

00:01:29.592 --> 00:01:32.320
Longer pushes the room away, shorter pulls it in.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:33.836
The width readout doesn't change.

00:01:34.216 --> 00:01:38.276
And this is SHARP's thirty millimetre default, for a photo with no EXIF.

00:01:39.758 --> 00:01:41.573
So the focal length matters.

00:01:41.573 --> 00:01:45.566
Depth Pro 2 ships a twenty-three point five million parameter estimator.

00:01:45.566 --> 00:01:49.558
On ordinary photos its median error was one point four percent.

00:01:49.558 --> 00:01:51.373
At the extremes it drifts.

00:01:51.373 --> 00:01:53.914
A fixed thirty millimetres is far worse.

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:59.933
I scored the largest model against Kinect depth on twenty-four frames, with

00:01:59.933 --> 00:02:01.294
the calibrated focal length.

00:02:01.694 --> 00:02:04.483
On average, eight point one percent relative error.

00:02:04.483 --> 00:02:06.227
But the scale isn't constant.

00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:11.014
Near things come out six percent close, far things seven percent far.

00:02:11.014 --> 00:02:13.208
At six metres, that's forty-two centimetres.

00:02:14.658 --> 00:02:17.463
SHARP is the splat model from the viral clip.

00:02:17.463 --> 00:02:21.516
It is Apple's, from December twenty twenty-five, built on the first Depth Pro.

00:02:21.896 --> 00:02:26.157
Two depth layers become two Gaussians per two-by-two pixels, and a

00:02:26.157 --> 00:02:27.706
decoder corrects every one.

00:02:27.706 --> 00:02:31.966
One million, one hundred seventy-nine thousand, six hundred forty-eight in all.

00:02:33.575 --> 00:02:36.141
Every Gaussian sits on a ray of the input camera.

00:02:36.521 --> 00:02:38.803
Move the camera, and rays slip behind edges.

00:02:38.803 --> 00:02:41.084
The second layer can fill some of that.

00:02:41.464 --> 00:02:43.820
Move further, and nothing was ever placed there.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:49.674
On my six photos, nine in ten second-layer Gaussians sit within one

00:02:49.674 --> 00:02:51.786
percent of the first layer's depth.

00:02:52.166 --> 00:02:54.275
And glass is a picture on a wall.

00:02:54.275 --> 00:02:55.856
The hills reflected here are flat.

00:02:57.283 --> 00:03:00.208
That's fine for what SHARP is for: a photo you can lean

00:03:00.208 --> 00:03:01.182
into, on a headset.

00:03:01.562 --> 00:03:05.265
Its depth scales exactly with the focal length it's handed, so the

00:03:05.265 --> 00:03:07.117
estimator is the piece worth adding.

00:03:08.325 --> 00:03:12.656
SplatLab edits splats of multi-view scans: label, lift out, fill the floor.

00:03:13.036 --> 00:03:16.121
From one photo there is no floor behind the table to find.

00:03:17.683 --> 00:03:19.247
One photo gives you the width.

00:03:19.247 --> 00:03:21.072
The focal length gives you the depth.

00:03:21.072 --> 00:03:23.157
Keep it, and record where it came from.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:28.221
Width from the photo, depth from the focal length.

00:03:28.221 --> 00:03:29.078
Thanks for watching!

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:32.280
Every source is in the full article.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:32.860
I'm Bokeh.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:33.151
Bye!
